WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.255 --> 00:00:03.088
[yells]

00:00:03.088 --> 00:00:04.920
A gymnast would always notice

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.906
if a screw or a nail were on the beam,

00:00:08.906 --> 00:00:10.210
'cause you're pretty much always

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.262
spotting where your hands and feet are.

00:00:12.262 --> 00:00:14.221
Hi, everyone. I'm Katelyn Ohashi,

00:00:14.221 --> 00:00:15.940
a four-time national team member

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.176
and a former UCLA gymnast.

00:00:18.176 --> 00:00:20.197
So, I just started my own YouTube channel,

00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:21.990
so you guys can check me out there.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.360
Today, I'm going to be
watching gymnastics movie clips

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.476
and rating them on their accuracy.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.373
I like that they added
not only flipping skills,

00:00:35.373 --> 00:00:36.490
but connecting skills,

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:39.160
'cause you need both for gymnastics.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.632
So, she did a roundoff back handspring,

00:00:41.632 --> 00:00:44.150
two and a half, punch front full.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.441
I used to do that skill
in my routine as well.

00:00:51.768 --> 00:00:56.520
The leaps are not necessarily
what you would see.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.730
I've always thought the
kicking parts were funny to me.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:03.055
Typically, if you're an elite gymnast

00:01:03.055 --> 00:01:04.995
you're so tired that you don't even

00:01:04.995 --> 00:01:06.617
have time to add those things.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.060
I do a double lay like that.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.670
We both have that part
in common, for sure.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.763
In 2006, I feel like gymnasts were

00:01:16.763 --> 00:01:19.830
more robotic than this movie portrayed,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:20.950
which is super awesome.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.440
So this type of routine
is way more accepted now

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:24.698
than it used to be.

00:01:28.243 --> 00:01:30.520
[laughing] I don't think I've ever seen

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.989
anyone do that in gymnastics,

00:01:32.989 --> 00:01:36.446
but it's incredible
and would be super fun.

00:01:36.446 --> 00:01:38.230
The dance moves are way different

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.071
than something you would see on beam.

00:01:44.394 --> 00:01:46.610
I don't know if I've ever seen someone

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.809
do a back handspring double back

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:51.951
just because there's not a lot of momentum

00:01:51.951 --> 00:01:53.600
out of a step-out back handspring.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.365
This was definitely less realistic

00:01:57.365 --> 00:02:00.580
gymnastics-wise as
well, besides the dance,

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.993
just because it was like,

00:02:01.993 --> 00:02:04.510
she definitely didn't have
all the requirements in there.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:07.270
She didn't have a leap pass or turn,

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.035
unless you want to count the head spin,

00:02:09.035 --> 00:02:10.419
'cause I would.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:12.040
I would say she would just need

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.520
more dance elements,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.430
even though she was
dancing the whole time,

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.612
but specific dance elements.

00:02:17.612 --> 00:02:20.620
So, I would give the
first one a 10 out of 10

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.459
and the second one probably a 3 out of 10.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:27.109
OK, so I've never cheered before, so what?

00:02:27.109 --> 00:02:28.530
Front handspring, step out,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.680
roundoff, back handspring,

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.370
step out, roundoff, back handspring,

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.004
full twisting layout.

00:02:34.004 --> 00:02:36.580
Every gymnast would
probably be able to do that,

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.996
but it's not anything our coaches

00:02:38.996 --> 00:02:40.746
would ever ask us to do.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.043
She started out with, like, a cartwheel,

00:02:51.043 --> 00:02:52.647
and then she turned around

00:02:52.647 --> 00:02:54.980
and did a front handspring.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.307
We never have cartwheels
in our tumbling passes.

00:02:58.307 --> 00:03:00.770
It'll be roundoffs, which is
the closest thing to that.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.412
You typically will turn
when you're in the air

00:03:04.412 --> 00:03:06.880
or not on your feet.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.960
Also she only took, like,
two steps into that,

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.678
which I understand some of
her skills built momentum,

00:03:11.678 --> 00:03:13.990
but we do a little more than two steps.

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.822
Probably at least three or four.

00:03:16.822 --> 00:03:19.190
It's a lot harder on a hardwood floor.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.277
We do have a little padding

00:03:21.277 --> 00:03:22.812
and a little bit of a spring.

00:03:22.812 --> 00:03:24.219
Props to cheerleaders,

00:03:24.219 --> 00:03:26.852
because they do a lot
of stuff that I'm like,

00:03:26.852 --> 00:03:28.927
I have no idea how they did it.

00:03:28.927 --> 00:03:33.060
So, for an accurate gymnastics pass,

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.077
that would probably be a zero.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.104
Amazing. [laughs]

00:03:47.104 --> 00:03:48.972
So, that was rhythmic gymnastics

00:03:48.972 --> 00:03:50.839
and not artistic gymnastics.

00:03:50.839 --> 00:03:53.410
I'm not too knowledgeable
on rhythmic gymnastics,

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:56.340
but obviously it's always on the floor,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.030
they're incredibly flexible,

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.700
they're coordinated,

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:01.890
which, most gymnasts,

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.350
artistic gymnastics are not coordinated.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.390
So, they take that ribbon,

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.060
and they throw it up,

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.850
they do something beautiful,

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.233
and then they catch it again.

00:04:11.233 --> 00:04:15.893
You know, he was getting pretty
good spins on his ribbon,

00:04:15.893 --> 00:04:18.617
but since rhythmic gymnastics

00:04:18.617 --> 00:04:21.817
is so beautiful and stunning,

00:04:21.817 --> 00:04:24.950
he didn't elongate his legs at all,

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:29.170
and he wasn't showing any flexibility.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:30.411
He was, like, galloping,

00:04:30.411 --> 00:04:33.012
versus actually doing something.

00:04:37.753 --> 00:04:42.170
I've never seen a gymnast
smoking on the rings.

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:45.632
Women don't do rings as an apparatus.

00:04:45.632 --> 00:04:47.558
That's a men's event.

00:04:47.558 --> 00:04:51.480
And typically you would
see them straight out.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.788
He was kind of down here,

00:04:52.788 --> 00:04:56.034
which would be even harder to hold.

00:04:56.034 --> 00:04:57.310
Just thinking about it,

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.260
I'm like, oh, his shoulders
are torn. [laughs]

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.890
And his chest, everything.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:05.550
Iron cross is near to impossible

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.990
unless you're, like, that trained in it.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.134
I'm gonna rate that probably a zero.

00:05:17.978 --> 00:05:18.811
[yells]

00:05:18.811 --> 00:05:20.618
A gymnast would always notice

00:05:20.618 --> 00:05:24.645
if a screw or a nail were on the beam,

00:05:24.645 --> 00:05:26.300
'cause you're pretty much always spotting

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.780
where your hands and feet are,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.741
and you're also just looking at the beam.

00:05:32.291 --> 00:05:36.151
Coach: Here we go, Candice. Stay loose.

00:05:36.151 --> 00:05:38.638
[yells]

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:40.739
[Katelyn laughs]

00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:43.100
That girl on bars was
doing a lot of giants,

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.375
and she needed to come off the bar

00:05:44.375 --> 00:05:47.290
a lot sooner than that anyways.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:48.950
The form, the giants were fine.

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:51.254
She did a little blind change in there.

00:05:51.254 --> 00:05:54.620
Also, I think that if a lot of chalk

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.133
is coming into your face,

00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:59.990
you would be able to close your eyes

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:02.960
and stop the momentum of your giant

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.270
and just kind of jump off after

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.242
instead of doing the
whole extra peeling thing.

00:06:08.242 --> 00:06:12.070
I think that it's goofy for the most part,

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:15.525
but it's more common than you'd think.

00:06:15.525 --> 00:06:17.390
Like, a lot of people --
we call it scorpion.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:22.383
So I've ended up like that a couple times

00:06:22.383 --> 00:06:24.550
from that height of that bar,

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:25.910
and it looks painful,

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:27.720
and it is painful.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.305
So, this is obviously a
little bit exaggerated,

00:06:30.305 --> 00:06:33.370
but gymnastics is definitely
a very dangerous sport,

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.420
and I know of people that have actually

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:37.910
lost their lives to the sport.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:40.775
And so it's something that

00:06:40.775 --> 00:06:42.980
you can't really let
your mind get loose in

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.093
no matter how comfortable
you are with a skill

00:06:45.093 --> 00:06:46.510
or how long you've been doing it.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.710
Granted, we learn how to
fall from a young age.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.540
So a lot of our falls are safer,

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:56.881
but you can still get into
stuff you didn't expect.

00:06:56.881 --> 00:06:59.480
What she was doing on bars,

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:02.408
it's not that hard to stop your momentum.

00:07:02.408 --> 00:07:07.280
She should have been a little
more prepared to stop them.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.100
I would give the gymnastics a 10.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.951
Obviously I think that fall is
very realistic in gymnastics,

00:07:13.951 --> 00:07:17.721
but how it happened is not realistic.

00:07:17.721 --> 00:07:18.979
Spider-Man: Oh, boy.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.076
I would say it was a little bit more

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:27.008
like cheerleading back handsprings,

00:07:27.008 --> 00:07:28.690
which, there's a very clear difference

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.610
between a cheerleader and gymnast.

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:32.070
Cheerleaders have more bent knees,

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.450
and Spider-Man definitely
had more bent knees.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:36.888
He was lower to the ground when he landed.

00:07:36.888 --> 00:07:38.610
Gymnastics is just very technical,

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.882
so if we were to have bent
knees in our back handsprings,

00:07:40.882 --> 00:07:42.100
we would get deductions.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.390
He was just trying to
do the back handsprings

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:44.223
and get out of the way,

00:07:44.223 --> 00:07:45.780
which I totally understand.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.715
It reminds me of this one girl,

00:07:48.715 --> 00:07:51.112
Danusia Francis, who used to go to UCLA,

00:07:51.112 --> 00:07:53.566
but she was about to fall

00:07:53.566 --> 00:07:56.029
out of one of her tumbling passes,

00:07:56.029 --> 00:07:58.150
and instead of stepping or falling,

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:00.250
she decided to do a back handspring,

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:02.300
and it kind of looked like Spider-Man's.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.317
I've always thought about, like,

00:08:03.317 --> 00:08:04.850
what would I do in a fight?

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.410
Would I be able to throw
out some gymnastics moves

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.657
and somehow hit them or dodge? [laughs]

00:08:10.657 --> 00:08:13.260
So, I don't know if anything
would really be that helpful.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.273
Maybe being able to kick high.

00:08:15.273 --> 00:08:18.928
'Cause I'm short, so I need
all the height I can get.

00:08:18.928 --> 00:08:21.094
We'll say an 8. We'll say an 8.

00:08:31.232 --> 00:08:32.315
She did an Arabian.

00:08:32.315 --> 00:08:34.312
That's what she did when she turned.

00:08:34.312 --> 00:08:37.807
And you would want to land
completely facing backwards,

00:08:37.807 --> 00:08:39.911
and then turn in the air

00:08:39.911 --> 00:08:44.357
and do a front flip to her
little handstand thing she did.

00:08:44.357 --> 00:08:45.799
She got good height on it.

00:08:45.799 --> 00:08:48.100
I think her form was pretty good.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:49.714
Yeah, everything looked really clean.

00:08:49.714 --> 00:08:51.320
Her knees were straight,

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.480
it looked like she had momentum

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:55.869
on her back handsprings for the most part.

00:08:55.869 --> 00:08:58.490
And she did enough to build that momentum

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:01.063
to be able to get that
height for the Arabian.

00:09:04.012 --> 00:09:06.326
We used to have to hold handstands

00:09:06.326 --> 00:09:08.830
for a minute without moving.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:11.925
I'm sure people could
do it longer than that.

00:09:11.925 --> 00:09:13.210
Especially if you're walking,

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:14.810
you can hold it for a long time,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.830
but minimum a minute.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.520
I would rate this a 9.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:20.803
She was actually pretty
good at gymnastics.

00:09:21.973 --> 00:09:22.806
Up here!

00:09:22.806 --> 00:09:23.639
Up here!

00:09:25.131 --> 00:09:26.206
Kelly, no!

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:35.256
That actually reminded me
of gymnastics in the '70s,

00:09:35.256 --> 00:09:38.630
because we used to have bars
that were way closer together,

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.330
so you would hit your hips on the bar.

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:42.741
That's how close they were.

00:09:42.741 --> 00:09:45.289
I'm very grateful for the change,

00:09:45.289 --> 00:09:47.300
because I feel like they definitely

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:49.900
always walked around with
bruises on their hips

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:52.290
and their pelvic bones.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:54.870
But gymnastics also got more difficult

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:56.960
when they spread the bars out wider,

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:01.546
because then you were
able to do more skills,

00:10:01.546 --> 00:10:04.083
release moves, certain stuff like that.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.090
I've kicked my coach on bars.

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:14.428
And I also know of just
people getting injured

00:10:14.428 --> 00:10:16.642
all the time from walking underneath.

00:10:16.642 --> 00:10:18.752
Could it kick over a dinosaur?

00:10:18.752 --> 00:10:21.016
If you had the intentions, maybe.

00:10:21.016 --> 00:10:22.759
That was a metal bar, right?

00:10:22.759 --> 00:10:25.433
And those don't give as much.

00:10:25.433 --> 00:10:27.241
But ours is wood.

00:10:27.241 --> 00:10:29.200
You don't use your muscles on bars.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:30.490
Except for holding,

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:32.095
your forearms and stuff like that.

00:10:32.095 --> 00:10:34.722
You want to use the bar as much

00:10:34.722 --> 00:10:39.310
to your own benefit as you can.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.004
Gymnastically, since it came out in '97,

00:10:43.004 --> 00:10:45.260
I'll rate it a

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.193
7.

00:10:54.301 --> 00:10:56.263
I don't think the leaps were that good.

00:10:56.263 --> 00:10:58.420
You know, some gymnasts
don't have great leaps.

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:01.627
Technically for choreography
there aren't points,

00:11:01.627 --> 00:11:03.280
but I think it only helps

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.700
if you can take the judge
out of their head so much

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.392
and want to just watch you

00:11:09.392 --> 00:11:12.227
so they're not constantly
just writing down deductions.

00:11:12.227 --> 00:11:13.290
Ariana: Focus.

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:15.270
Two flips with a full twist,

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:17.060
push off right leg,

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:20.010
and then tumble through the roundoff.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.505
What I actually really
enjoy about that movie

00:11:22.505 --> 00:11:25.730
is that it goes into her thought process

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:27.220
when she's about to tumble,

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:31.795
and I think that most gymnasts have cues

00:11:31.795 --> 00:11:33.210
that they give themselves

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.277
every single time that they go.

00:11:35.277 --> 00:11:38.003
And so hers was like,
"Push off your back leg,"

00:11:38.003 --> 00:11:39.940
"strong," certain stuff like that.

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:41.650
So it's these little reminders

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:45.160
that just help us get through the pass

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.890
and stay focused.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.090
So, I actually think that
I've competed in that leo.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.006
Some leotards that I was wearing,

00:11:51.006 --> 00:11:53.880
the elastic would be worn out,

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:58.865
and that's when we'd have to get the spray

00:11:58.865 --> 00:12:02.560
and use it to stick our leos down.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.710
We'll give it, we'll give it a 10.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:08.752
I know, I'm not biased
or anything. [laughs]

00:12:15.994 --> 00:12:18.314
She definitely had leg separation

00:12:18.314 --> 00:12:21.230
on her path to the low bar.

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:23.213
She also, if you want
to get really technical,

00:12:23.213 --> 00:12:25.516
she didn't hit her handstands.

00:12:25.516 --> 00:12:28.300
The bars were much closer in the '70s

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.160
than that clip showed.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:32.922
For the '70s, I would say that was

00:12:32.922 --> 00:12:35.906
very accurate for the skill level,

00:12:35.906 --> 00:12:38.060
besides the bars being

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:41.493
a different length away from each other.

00:12:46.416 --> 00:12:49.600
That isn't that common
to see that dismount,

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:51.830
but it does still exist today.

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:53.296
It's just pretty rare,

00:12:53.296 --> 00:12:56.268
and you would see it
more through that period,

00:12:56.268 --> 00:12:58.589
but everything was kind of the same.

00:12:58.589 --> 00:13:01.572
Like, all those skills
you still see today.

00:13:01.572 --> 00:13:05.350
She definitely had deductions
within her routine.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:08.170
When she landed, she
obviously stumbled around.

00:13:08.170 --> 00:13:10.270
And she did have a release move.

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:13.696
But you'll see multiple release
moves and spinning moves

00:13:13.696 --> 00:13:15.588
if you're trying to go to the Olympics.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:22.726
I'm giving that a 10 for accuracy

00:13:22.726 --> 00:13:25.617
just because of the way
the coach responded.

00:13:25.617 --> 00:13:27.833
[laughs]

00:13:27.833 --> 00:13:30.270
It's funny because that was in Russia,

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:32.010
and I had Russian coaches,

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.300
and I would say Russian coaches

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:37.010
tend to be pretty brutal. [laughs]

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:38.400
And a lot of coaches are,

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:39.652
so if we just look at
each other, we're like,

00:13:39.652 --> 00:13:41.672
"Oh, yeah, our coach is
Russian," we're like,

00:13:41.672 --> 00:13:45.652
ah, we understand exactly
how training was, then.

00:13:45.652 --> 00:13:47.222
I thought it was a 10, because you still

00:13:47.222 --> 00:13:49.530
see all of those skills today.

00:13:54.877 --> 00:13:57.627
[crowd cheering]

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:00.130
So, she does the hands,

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:00.963
her feet go over,

00:14:00.963 --> 00:14:02.110
and then she does an extra flip

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:03.500
and lands back on her feet.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:06.006
That first one is very accurate.

00:14:06.006 --> 00:14:09.640
So, in college we would have a natty stick

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:10.820
and a college stick.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:13.940
The college stick is more
acting like you stuck it.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:15.660
Like, if you know you're gonna step,

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:18.233
it's like stepping and
just saluting to the judges

00:14:18.233 --> 00:14:21.090
immediately, and that's
more of a college stick.

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:24.876
Whereas a natty stick is
being able to hold it,

00:14:24.876 --> 00:14:26.626
put your feet together, salute,

00:14:26.626 --> 00:14:29.099
and then turn and salute the judges.

00:14:34.241 --> 00:14:35.430
[crowd cheering]

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:38.130
I have no idea what that second vault was,

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:41.700
because it didn't even look
like she flipped in it.

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:43.777
It just looked like she was twisting.

00:14:43.777 --> 00:14:45.836
And so I flinch, 'cause
I'm like, wait a minute,

00:14:45.836 --> 00:14:48.060
is she supposed to now do a flip?

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:49.800
The second one,

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.648
it would probably be worth nothing.

00:14:52.648 --> 00:14:54.632
[laughs] She didn't flip.

00:14:54.632 --> 00:14:59.329
That's actually based on a true story.

00:14:59.329 --> 00:15:02.854
Kerri Strug, who went to the Olympics

00:15:02.854 --> 00:15:04.937
and broke her foot.

00:15:04.937 --> 00:15:06.415
She had to go again,

00:15:06.415 --> 00:15:08.415
and she stuck on one leg.

00:15:08.415 --> 00:15:09.648
So, my freshman year,

00:15:09.648 --> 00:15:13.582
I was competing on beam,

00:15:13.582 --> 00:15:14.790
I finished the routine,

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.556
but when I did my dismount,

00:15:16.556 --> 00:15:19.000
my foot slipped off and
I landed on my head.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.543
What I didn't know is
I fractured my sternum.

00:15:21.543 --> 00:15:23.270
I'm getting tested for a concussion,

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:25.650
and they told me that there
was "equipment malfunction,"

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.140
so I got to go again.

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:29.750
And I remember doing a skill on the floor

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:31.008
before I went again, and I was like,

00:15:31.008 --> 00:15:33.145
oh, wow, my chest really hurts.

00:15:33.145 --> 00:15:35.320
But I still did the full beam routine,

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:37.620
did the dismount again, got home,

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:40.153
found out that I had fractured my sternum.

00:15:40.153 --> 00:15:42.192
[laughs]

00:15:42.192 --> 00:15:45.930
So, I would rate the first clip a 10,

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:50.331
and then the second
clip, I'll rate it a 4.

00:15:50.331 --> 00:15:51.318
I feel like I'm definitely doing

00:15:51.318 --> 00:15:52.442
really bad on these ratings.

00:15:52.442 --> 00:15:53.540
I'm, like, going up.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.133
But this is how it goes.

00:15:55.133 --> 00:15:57.013
So, in gymnastics too,

00:15:57.013 --> 00:15:58.750
the later you go in the lineup,

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:01.423
the higher score you're
probably gonna get.

